# PN (Paradigm Networks) login check

This plugin (`pn-sanitizer`) gates every prompt and tool call through CodeDefense,
which requires this workspace to be logged in to PN. Two other mechanisms try to
surface a missing login, but **neither reliably reaches you (the agent) on the
first message of a session**, so this rule is the backstop:

- `check-session.py` (`sessionStart`) injects context asking you to request the
  base URL, but `sessionStart` is fire-and-forget — the agent loop doesn't wait
  for or enforce it, so it can race with (or miss) the user's first message.
- `check-prompt.py` (`beforeSubmitPrompt`) only supports `continue` /
  `user_message` output fields. `user_message` is a UI-level notice to the
  human when a prompt is blocked — it is never injected into your context, so
  an "allowed" prompt with PN unconfigured gives you no signal to act on.

## What to do

On the **first user message of a session**, before doing anything else, check
whether PN is already configured. Do this with a plain shell command — do
**not** try to locate this plugin's own `scripts/` directory to import
`pn_config.py`; the plugin can be installed anywhere (`~/.cursor/plugins/...`)
and there is no environment variable that tells you where, so path-guessing
is unreliable. The credentials file lives at a fixed, well-known path
regardless of install location:

```bash
if [ -f ~/.pn/credentials.json ] || { [ -n "$SNANTIZER_BASE_URL" ] && [ -n "$SNANTIZER_TOKEN" ]; }; then echo CONFIGURED; else echo NOT_CONFIGURED; fi
```

- `CONFIGURED` → PN already has a stored login (or a shared-host env override
  is set). Proceed with the user's request normally — no further action
  needed. (This only checks presence, not validity — an expired token that
  fails to refresh is already handled by `check-prompt.py`/`check-response.py`
  failing open/closed on their own, so don't try to validate the token here.)
- `NOT_CONFIGURED` → PN is **not** configured. You must invoke the
  `AskQuestion` tool itself to ask for the base URL — actually call the tool,
  do not just type the prompt and options into a chat message as text or a
  bulleted list. Writing it out in prose produces none of the clickable UI or
  free-text "Other" field; it just looks like a plain question to the user,
  which is exactly what this is meant to avoid. Call it with:
  - prompt: "What's your PN base URL? (e.g. `https://acme.paradigmnetworks.ai`)"
  - options: `"I'm not sure what my base URL is"`, `"I think I'm already logged in — recheck"`
  - the user's own URL comes back via the automatic "Other" option; do not
    invent, guess, or reuse a base URL from another project or example.
    Validate it once, correct at most one mistake, then move on — see the
    `pn-login` skill for the full flow, including what to do if it still
    doesn't look like a URL after that.

  Once you have it, run the `pn-login` skill to log in before proceeding
  with their original request.

Do not treat an allowed prompt or a "CodeDefense API unreachable" message as
proof that PN is configured — `check-prompt.py` and `check-response.py` fail
open on purpose, so prompts and tool calls can proceed even when there's no
login at all.

You only need to do this once per session — after you've confirmed PN is
configured (or completed a login), don't repeat the check on later messages
unless the user asks to log in again or a hook message reports the login
expired.
